================================================================================
Epilogue: Langston After the Wolf
================================================================================

Adaptation direction
--------------------
This epilogue closes the Wolves of Langston contract without removing Langston or
its people from the open world. It is not a farewell, a final visit, or the start
of another investigation. Tomlin, Roi, Mayor Anathram, Solaine, the Pickled Hen,
the temple, and the town remain available after it through their normal locations.

The original paired-garland investigation, second search of the temple, mysterious
replacement cleric, Solaine's escape, and shadow on the road are not part of this
ending. If any of those ideas are ever reused, they should begin a separately named
optional quest after the Wolves contract has already been completed.


[360]
When you next pass through Langston's market square, the town is busy with ordinary
work again. The stains from the accusation have been scrubbed from the cobbles.
Stalls are open, neighbors have returned to arguing over prices, and children are
once more being shooed away from the well.

People still recognize you. Some offer thanks, some stare, and others make a point
of treating you as though nothing unusual happened. Langston has not forgotten the
wolf in its square, but neither has it stopped living because of her.

A clerk from the Hall catches your attention near the entrance.

"Mayor Anathram asked me to tell you that she and Roi have an account of everything
that followed," she says. "She said there is no urgency. They will receive you when
you have the time."

This follow-up remains available from the Langston Hall until completed. Declining
it now returns the player to the market square and does not close any location.

Choices:
- Call on Mayor Anathram and Roi now: go to 361
- Continue with your business in Langston and return another time: return to the
  Langston market square


[361]
Mayor Anathram receives you in her office. The shutters and lamps reflect the current
hour, and whatever weather lies over Langston can be heard faintly beyond the walls.
Roi occupies the armchair beside her desk, with Buttercup settled wherever she has
found the greatest inconvenience for everyone else.

"The murder of Maybelline is solved," the mayor says. "Solaine is in custody, her
curse is broken, and the town has begun putting its affairs back in order. You
deserve to know what followed."

Roi nods. "Healing, testimony, repairs, and a great deal of paperwork. Quiet work,
mostly, though no less necessary."

The mayor opens a folder containing reports from the militia, the temple, and the
people caring for the wounded.

"There are several matters you deserve to hear about," she says.

Choices:
- Ask how Tomlin and the wounded militia members are recovering: go to 362
- Ask what will happen to Solaine: go to 363
- Ask whether Langston is safe without its True Sun cleric: go to 364
- Ask about Maybelline's family and the town's dead: go to 365
- Finish the meeting and return to the market square: go to 366


[362]
"Tomlin lives," Roi says. "He is awake, eating, complaining about the food, and
trying to convince everyone that he can walk farther than the length of his room."

The mayor's mouth tightens in what might be relief. "The militia members injured in
the square are also expected to recover. Their families have them under close watch,
which may be a harsher sentence than bed rest."

Tomlin will remain under care until he is fit to return to his cottage. He is not
removed from the game: while he recovers he can be visited where he is staying, and
afterward he resumes his ordinary place in Langston. His later conversations can
acknowledge whether the player tended him in the temple courtyard.

"He knows who kept him alive," Roi adds. "Once he is steady enough for visitors, I
expect he'll want to thank you himself."

Choices:
- Ask about another part of the aftermath: return to 361
- Finish the meeting and return to the market square: go to 366


[363]
"Solaine remains human," the mayor says. "The Silverstar broke the curse. It did not
erase what she chose to do while she carried it."

The jail cell has been repaired and reinforced. Two militia members remain on watch,
with shifts arranged so that no exhausted guard is left alone. Solaine has been
given plain clothes, food, water, and treatment for the wounds she suffered below
the temple.

"Sometimes she answers questions," Roi says. "Sometimes she prays. Sometimes she
just sits there. She has not transformed again, and she has made no attempt to flee."

Mayor Anathram has sent a complete report to the Church of the True Sun and to its
Order of Radiant Light, but she has not surrendered custody of Solaine merely
because the Church claims her as one of its own. Langston will hear what the Church
proposes, and Solaine will answer for Maybelline's murder.

Solaine remains a visitable character at the town jail. Her later conversations may
change as the town decides how she will be tried or transferred, but those are
continuing world events, not unfinished requirements of the Wolves contract.

Choices:
- Ask about another part of the aftermath: return to 361
- Finish the meeting and return to the market square: go to 366


[364]
"Veyra's ward did not vanish the moment Solaine was removed," Roi says. "It is
weakened, not gone. The Church of the True Sun assures us that a new caretaker
from the Order of Radiant Light can arrive before that becomes dangerous."

Until then, the temple and its mortuary are being watched. Roi has organized the
militia and several volunteers into shifts, and no body awaiting burial is left
unattended. The Order of Radiant Light has acknowledged the mayor's message and
confirmed that a new caretaker is already on the road.

"We are taking precautions," Mayor Anathram says. "If the Church keeps its word, the
ward will have a keeper well before it fails. If it does not, Langston will decide
what must be done."

The temple remains available as a Langston location. Its new caretaker can arrive as
a later settlement event, without reopening or delaying completion of this quest.

Choices:
- Ask about another part of the aftermath: return to 361
- Finish the meeting and return to the market square: go to 366


[365]
Mayor Anathram has informed Maybelline's parents that her killer was identified and
captured. Roi has offered to speak with them about Maybelline's burial and any rites
they still want observed. Neither asks the player to carry another message or reopen
the investigation.

"They have lost a daughter," Roi says. "Answers do not make that loss smaller. But
they know the town did not forget her, and they know the person responsible will
answer for it."

Fresh flowers have appeared at Maybelline's grave. Some were left by her parents,
some by people from town, and some by hands nobody saw. Their colors brighten the
raw earth, and more appear whenever the older stems begin to wilt.

The cemetery and Maybelline's grave remain available for later visits if the player
chooses. Visiting is an act of remembrance, not a final obligation.

Choices:
- Ask about another part of the aftermath: return to 361
- Finish the meeting and return to the market square: go to 366


[366]
When there is nothing more you wish to ask, Mayor Anathram closes the folder.

"Then the account is settled," she says. "Langston owes you its thanks."

Roi pushes himself out of the armchair. "You'll find me at the grove, usually. At
the Hen if the day has been difficult."

Mayor Anathram has opened the next folder on her desk before you reach the door.
Buttercup follows you as far as the threshold, then returns to her mat at a word from
the mayor.

You step back into the market square. A hawker is arguing over the price of onions,
the well rope creaks, and the ordinary noise of Langston closes around you.

[b]The Wolves of Langston is complete.[/b]

Open-world handoff:
- Return to Langston's market square.
- Keep Mayor Anathram and Roi available through their usual locations and schedules.
- Keep Tomlin visitable during recovery, then return him to his cottage and workroom.
- Keep Solaine visitable at the jail while her custody state develops.
- Allow the new True Sun caretaker from the Order of Radiant Light to arrive later
  as a settlement event rather than an automatic continuation of this quest.


Implementation constraints for the later ChoiceScript adaptation
---------------------------------------------------------------
- Mark the Wolves quest complete and pay its reward before this follow-up becomes
  available. Nothing here can unset or delay completion.
- Do not force a Long Rest, advance to a particular day, or assume how long the player
  waited before returning.
- Use the live time, weather, and settlement ambience. Do not prescribe rain, dawn,
  dusk, or a fixed number of days since the accusation.
- The follow-up is a one-time Hall conversation that can be postponed without penalty.
- Every informational branch returns to the same aftermath menu. It grants no unique
  loot and starts no replacement investigation.
- Returning from the conversation goes to the normal Langston hub. It does not send
  the player onto the road or ask whether they are leaving town forever.
- Do not use an "epilogue open" flag to imply the Wolves quest remains unfinished.
  If a tracking flag is needed, it should mean only that this optional aftermath
  conversation has been seen.
- Any future garland, Church of the True Sun, Veyra, Order of Radiant Light, or Solaine
  storyline must register as a separate quest or settlement event with its own
  beginning and resolution.
